## 3. Supplied material and what it establishes

**Iris, OpenML 61** (checked 2026-10-17 by `story-repo`):
- Metadata: `https://www.openml.org/api/v1/json/data/61`; version 1, status active, licence "Public",
  default target `class`, creator R.A. Fisher (1936), original source UCI.
- ARFF: `https://www.openml.org/data/v1/download/61/iris.arff` (`https://openml.org/...` answers 301 to this URL;
  follow redirects). Downloaded file MD5 `ad484452702105cbf3d30f8deaba39a9`, equal to the `md5_checksum` OpenML lists.
  Parquet alternative: `https://data.openml.org/datasets/0000/0061/dataset_61.pq` (not checksummed by me).
- Content (measured on that ARFF): 150 rows, 4 real features (`sepallength`, `sepalwidth`, `petallength`,
  `petalwidth`, cm), target `class` with 3 classes x 50 rows (Iris-setosa, Iris-versicolor, Iris-virginica).
- OpenML description: setosa is linearly separable from the other two; versicolor and virginica are not linearly
  separable from each other; "an exceedingly simple domain".
- Consequences for the study: with stratified 5-fold CV every test fold has 30 rows (10 per class), so every fold
  accuracy is a multiple of 1/30; both methods are expected to be near the ceiling, so many paired fold differences
  may be exactly 0 and the difference interval may well include 0. This is a property to report, not to avoid.
  Iris contains duplicate feature rows, so kNN distance ties can occur (relevant to R10 reproducibility).

**Template: NeurIPS 2026** (`https://media.neurips.cc/Conferences/NeurIPS2026/Formatting_Instructions_For_NeurIPS_2026.zip`,
